\DOC X86_QUICKSTEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_QUICKSTEP_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> term list -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes a single x86 instruction in an {eventually}-style goal,
advancing the current state variable to a freshly named successor state.

\DESCRIBE
The argument {th : thm * (thm option array)} pairs the code-length theorem for
the loaded byte list with a cached array of per-instruction decoding theorems,
used to step the machine; {pats : term list} is a list of state-component
patterns controlling which reads are selected/retained for the new state;
{s : string} is the name to give the new state variable produced by the step.
The goal's assumptions {A} describe the current state {t}: code loaded, stack
pointer, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state, and the
current instruction pointer. Each assumption mentioning {t} is dropped and
replaced by the corresponding assumption about the new state {u} named by {s},
the instruction-pointer assumption being updated to the next instruction
address and the {MAYCHANGE} assumption re-expressed between the initial state
and {u} (its component list may grow where the stepped instruction writes
further components); assumptions not mentioning the state, such as
nonoverlapping side conditions, are left untouched.
{
   A ?- eventually x86 P t
   ======================================  X86_QUICKSTEP_TAC th pats s
   A' ?- eventually x86 P u
}
\noindent where {u} is the state named by {s} and {A'} consists of the
untouched assumptions together with the state-dependent assumptions transferred
to {u}.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-step, one-subgoal behaviour, always
on goals of this shape with a state-describing assumption set.

\ENDDOC
